  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Family Reunification and Re-integreation of Streetchildren in Kinshasa (DRC)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">20,000 girls and boys, many of whom are branded as witches, end up on city streets in the Democratic Republic of Congo. They face daily risk of abuse, drug-addiction and violence. Project staff meet them on the streets, both by day and night, and encourage local leaders to help and refer children. At a drop-in centre, staff listen to them and offer medical care, a meal, washing facilities, literacy classes and games. There is a temporary shelter for girls. The project traces the families and works with them to address their problems so the children can return home. It helps the children go back to school or learn a vocational skill. Staff visit families regularly, offer them small loans to start income-generating activities and help with their rent. The project organises meetings with local church leaders, government authorities and the community, as well as broadcasting radio programmes, to tackle people&apos;s attitudes and behaviour to the children &#x2013; and especially the belief in child witchcraft. By the end of three years, the children will be living back at home, going to school or have a trade. Their family and community will combat accusations of witchcraft and protect them.</narrative>
  </description>
